---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { groupsEnabled } from '../lib/flags';

if (!groupsEnabled()) {
  return Astro.redirect('/');
}

// Public, static: the transparent-ranking page (PLAN.md §2, §7).
const { user } = Astro.locals;
---

<Layout title="How ranking works — Radar" user={user}>
  <h1>How ranking works</h1>
  <p>
    Most discovery systems reward accumulated attention, which entrenches whoever is
    already big. This one is built to surface new and small sources alongside popular
    ones, and this page documents exactly how. There is no engagement tuning and no
    personalization; everyone in a group sees the same edition.
  </p>

  <h2>The score</h2>
  <p>Within a group's edition window:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>r(L)</code> — distinct members who recommended link L.</li>
    <li><code>m</code> — members active in the window (floored at 3 so tiny groups don't produce wild numbers).</li>
    <li><code>p(L) = r(L) / m</code> — the base signal is a <em>proportion</em>, not a total, so nothing accumulates and the mechanic reads the same at 12 members and 12,000.</li>
    <li><code>a(s)</code> — recommendations of source s across the whole system in the last 90 days; <code>A</code> — all recommendations in that time.</li>
    <li><code>w(s) = min(ln((1+A)/(1+a(s))), 4)</code> — an inverse-frequency weight: a recommendation of a rarely-seen source counts for more.</li>
    <li><code>score(L) = p(L) × (1 + 0.3·w(s))</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Composing an edition</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Per-source cap.</strong> At most one link per source per edition (a group owner can raise it to two).</li>
    <li><strong>Reserved slots.</strong> About 20% of the edition (at least one slot) is reserved for sources that have never appeared in the group before, or whose <code>a(s)</code> is in the bottom quarter system-wide. Those slots are filled by score within that pool.</li>
    <li><strong>Remaining slots</strong> go by score across everything eligible.</li>
    <li><strong>Minimum evidence.</strong> A link needs two distinct recommenders, or one in groups with fewer than six active members.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Labels</h2>
  <p>
    Every surfaced link carries exactly one label saying why it is there:
    <em>First time here</em> (source never seen in this group),
    <em>Small source</em> (bottom quarter system-wide),
    <em>Rising</em> (most of its recommendations arrived in the last third of the window), or
    <em>Hot this week</em> (everything else, including the most-recommended link).
    When a link qualifies for several, the first in that order wins.
  </p>

  <h2>Damping promotion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Duplicate URLs collapse into one canonical link.</li>
    <li>Accounts whose recent recommendations are mostly one source count for half.</li>
    <li>Per-account daily save limits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The public layer</h2>
  <p>
    A link appears on <a href="/highlights">Highlights</a> only when at least three
    <em>independent</em> groups (member overlap under 30%) recommended it in the same
    month, from groups that opted into contributing. The public layer shows counts,
    never names.
  </p>

  <h2>Is it working?</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/dashboard">dashboard</a> publishes the measurements that matter: how
    often new sources surface, how concentrated edition slots are in the top sources,
    and how many distinct sources an edition carries. If the window is closing on this
    platform too, the dashboard will show it.
  </p>
</Layout>
